# Add a book to the bookshelf

## Scope

- Read **`_data/books/book-schema.json`** once — do not copy enums into this rule.
- Edit **one** list file: `_data/books/[YEAR]bookList.json`. Default to the **newest year** unless the user names a year or file (e.g. `0000bookList.json`).
- **Append** one object before `]`. Match **field order and style** of that file’s last entry.
- Conform to the schema. If a tag is missing, add it to `book-schema.json` in the same change.
- Do **not** search the repo, other year files, publisher/indie product pages, or run `npm`.

**Always write:** `cover`, `coverAlt`, `link`, `title`, `subTitle` (`""` if none), `author`, `type`, `tags`, `status`, `description` (`""` unless the user gives notes). Optional: `graphic`, `graphicAlt` under `/assets/images/`.

## Covers

Prefer **Open Library**, then **Amazon**. Never use publisher, indie, or storefront image URLs (HarperCollins, O’Reilly, Penguin, author sites, etc.).

1. If the user gives an Open Library, Amazon, or local `/assets/images/` cover — use it. No search.
2. Else get an ISBN (from the user’s link path, or one Open Library/ISBN search). Try:

   `https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/isbn/{ISBN}-L.jpg?default=false`

   HEAD it. Use the URL **without** `?default=false` in JSON only if the HEAD is `200` and not a tiny placeholder.
3. If Open Library 404s, times out, or is a 1×1 — Amazon `m.media-amazon.com` cover.
4. If both fail — **stop**. Ask the user to drop a file in `content/assets/images/` and use `/assets/images/…`. Do not scrape another host.

Do not fetch JS storefront pages for metadata. Write `coverAlt` from the edition you actually used.

## User message shape

```
Title: …
Author: …
Status: want-to-read
Tags: tag1, tag2
Link: …
Cover: … (Open Library, Amazon, local path, or omit)
Notes: … (or none)
```

Batch: numbered lines, same fields; one append per book, same file.
